where xi referred to departure
hour.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:14.200
That's what we were used to.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:15.700
But now,

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:19.840
we have multiple input
variables.

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.720
So we can't just say a single h
of xi.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.120
I might say h of xi 1,

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:29.120
h of xi 2.

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:33.380
But this seems a little silly,
because what if I have 50 input

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:34.160
variables?

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:37.910
What's the more convenient way
to write this?

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.700
Instead of in here having to
write out,

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:44.700
comma,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.480
all my other input variables,
what would I do?

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:47.880
Just write like a vector.

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:53.130
Yeah, why don't I make a vector
xi?

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.280
And what is this going to
return?

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.720
Well, it's going to return.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.860
I can still have an intercept.
Except I can still have one

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:10.280
parameter multiplied by the
first input variable.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:11.520
So this,

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.480
as we saw in the previous page,

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.230
would now be departure hour.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:23.580
But I'll now also add w2 times
xi2.

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:28.080
And this is day of month.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:41.820
What we are doing now is called
multiple linear

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:42.820
regression.

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:54.060
Why? Well, it's because we have
multiple features.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.240
So the question is,

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:05.990
how do we find optimal
parameters now?

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.560
How do you think we find optimal
parameters now? We have three

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:13.060
optimal parameters.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:19.970
How do we find w naught star,

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.880
w1 star, and w2 star?

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.880
How do we do?

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.380
Would we still be able to use
the same formula for w star

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:44.640
where it's x transpose x inverse
times x transpose y?

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.360
Somehow we need to be able to
relate this to the stuff we saw

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.920
a few moments ago, where we have
x transpose x inverse x

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:51.960
transpose y.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:56.020
The question is, what is the x?
Like, what do I put in my matrix

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:57.020
X?

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:00.360
My observation vector y, nothing
about that has really changed,

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.080
right? The things I am trying to
predict,

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:04.840
the actual commute times,

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:06.540
nothing has changed about those.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:09.880
What has changed is what goes in
my design matrix, because I'm

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.920
using more information to
actually make the prediction.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:16.710
So that design matrix itself
should also change,

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.340
OK?

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:24.260
So how do we do it? Well, let me
copy this

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:25.260
over,

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:36.860
and let me bring back the

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:38.360
example dataset.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:44.470
Our parameter vector now,

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:50.060
how many things are in it?

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.040
Not a trick question.
How many things are in the

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.560
parameter vector now?

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.560
Three.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.820
So that design matrix x,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.570
what should its shape be?

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:10.320
Yeah.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:13.220
We add another column.
Exactly.

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:17.140
So it has n rows like before.
It just has three columns.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:21.100
Can you tell me what the first
two rows of the design matrix

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:23.350
here would be?

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:33.960
What are the first two rows of
the design matrix?

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.740
Someone tell me the first row.

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.700
I should have three columns, so
I'm asking for three numbers

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:39.700
here.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.340
What are those three numbers?

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:42.340
Ones.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:44.500
I'm asking for the first row,
not the first column.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:45.160
First row.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:46.700
Yeah.
Yeah, so 1 is the first thing,

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:47.840
yeah?

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:50.720
Then, yeah, go for it.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.180
10.81
and 15.

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:56.320
Awesome.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:20:59.980
It includes all of the input
information for the first row of

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.420
our data set.

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:04.040
The rows of this design matrix
correspond to different data

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:04.740
points.

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:07.820
The columns correspond to
different features or input

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:08.820
variables.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.960
What's the second row?

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:13.360
Let me just highlight this.

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:16.520
The second row will be 1,

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:18.280
7.75,
and 16.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:38.480
First two rows of design matrix.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.340
So once we define this design
matrix and we have our

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:49.180
observation vector y, which just
has all of the actual commute

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:51.100
times like it did before,

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:53.000
finding the optimal parameters

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.750
is no different than before.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.980
Now, that's the cool thing.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:13.380
We already have solved, in
general,

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:17.480
for what the optimal parameters
will be for a model that is made

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:20.480
up of terms parameter

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.620
times feature plus parameter
times feature.

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:25.980
That's how we've defined our
model here.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:27.660
Parameter times feature,

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.910
parameter times feature.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.200
By the way, this particular
model,

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:35.950
what does it look like?

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:44.380
If I were to graph it out, what
would it look like?

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:48.020
Once I find my optimal w0, w1,
and w2,

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:53.580
if I had to draw it, what would
it look like?

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:54.540
Any guesses?

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:54.980
Yeah?

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:56.780
You mean you can still use a
line?

00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:59.380
Not quite, because now we have
two input variables.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:02.900
This model should give me a
prediction for my commute time,

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:06.650
no matter what departure hour
and day of month I pick.

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:09.660
So imagine the floor here,

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.780
one axis is departure hour, and
the other axis is day of the

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:13.260
month.

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:16.160
There should be a prediction for
my commute time above every

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:17.160
point.

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.160
So we should be thinking in 3D.

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:22.560
And what shape will this be in
3D? It'll be a plane.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:24.340
Yeah, exactly.
It'll be a plane in 3D.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:28.220
If you look at chapter 7.2,

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:33.140
we can see an equation of this
plane,

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:35.260
something like this.

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:39.760
I don't know why it's so
rotated, but you get the idea.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:45.790
So now we've found a plane of
best fit.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.790
Here's a question.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:09.180
is this plane a subspace of

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:10.180
R3?

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:13.940
Why?

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:16.640
It doesn't necessarily contain
the zero vector.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:17.160
Yeah, good.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.280
So remember,

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:23.040
a plane in R3 is a subspace of
R3 when it passes through the

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.040
origin.

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:26.860
This doesn't necessarily pass
through the origin,

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.540
so it's not necessarily a
subspace.

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:34.260
It only is a subspace if the
optimal w0

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:36.000
ends up being 0.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.680
And if that happened, then this
would be a subspace.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:39.640
Otherwise,

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:44.140
it would be what's called an
affine subspace,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:51.100
which is a subspace if it were
shifted to contain the origin.

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:52.080
But the shape is correct.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.720
Yeah? Is there a world where w0
is not 0 and it still passes to

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:57.220
the origin?

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:05.730
No, because suppose that were
true.

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:08.540
If this was like 7,

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:12.320
you plug in 0 here and 0 here,
the output would be 7, not 0.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:14.360
Because x and y have to both be
0.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:14.880
Yeah,

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:18.100
if you think of this as your x
and this as your y.

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.100
Yeah,

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:20.480
good.
OK,

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:24.860
so adding new features is pretty
straightforward.

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:27.520
All you have to do is just add
new things to your design

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:28.520
matrix.

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:34.480
So how far can this go?

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:38.420
This is not a line,

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:45.200
but can I find this degree 10
polynomial,

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:49.700
the one that minimizes mean
squared error, Or can I find it

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:53.450
using the ideas we've just
discovered?

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:59.960
Can I find the coefficients of
the x to the 10 and

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:03.740
x to the 9 and x to the 8 terms?
Can I find all of that using

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:05.320
linear regression?

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:13.030
And if I were to, how would I do
that?

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:18.670
How would I do that?

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:48.640
All right, something seems a
little weird because I'm saying

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.980
this is linear regression.
It seems to be about lines and

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:53.420
planes.
But how can you find this curve?

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:56.000
Yeah?

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:57.240
Y degree 10?

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:01.740
I just picked a random degree.

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:06.800
Well, let's think about what the
equation of this polynomial

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:08.300
looks like.

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:13.660
Its equation is going to be
something like h of xi

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:21.060
is equal to w0 plus w1 xi

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:25.620
plus w2 xi squared.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:29.040
Notice I didn't write
parentheses around this.

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:32.120
I'm not saying this I could
have, because it is my second

00:27:32.120 --> 00:27:36.060
feature in a way in which you
are about to see.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:38.760
But here I do actually mean xi
squared.

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:40.480
Whereas over here,

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:44.230
I did not mean squared.

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:51.560
But now I'm saying the features
in my model

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:56.340
are the original data points
raised to the power of 2 or 3

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:59.400
or all the way to the 10.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:02.040
That is what my model looks
like.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:05.620
Here's the connection.
You can find these optimal w's

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:09.060
using linear regression.
you just have to treat

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:14.660
these x's raised to the powers
as new

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:18.620
features that you create before
putting information into the

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:20.120
design matrix.

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:23.860
Here's the bigger idea.
As long as you can store the

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:28.300
input variables in your model in
a design matrix, you can put

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:29.800
whatever you want in the design
matrix.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:34.300
It doesn't need to be linear
operations of the original data.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:37.160
So the design matrix here,

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:39.460
we'll still have a column of
1's, because remember this

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:42.480
column of 1's is there anytime
we have an intercept term.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:44.220
That's why it was there.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:48.720
The next column can contain my
x's.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:53.640
My next column can just contain
the x's squared.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:56.460
Nothing wrong with that.

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:00.960
The next column can contain the
x's cubed.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:09.130
And I have a column for each
power.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:16.500
So this is an n by 11 design
matrix.

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:20.440
Now,

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:23.880
here I happen to choose a
polynomial example just to show

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:25.300
you that it's possible.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:29.060
But any one of these columns
could be replaced with whatever

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.040
function of x I want it to be.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:34.320
So this column with the x's
cubed,

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:40.080
instead I could replace this
with sine of

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:40.720
x1,

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:41.680
sine of x2,

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:42.920
sine of x3.

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:46.200
It could be whatever I want them
to be.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:48.360
So what is the limitation here?

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:53.060
What models can I produce with
multiple linear regression? In

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:53.480
addition,

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:57.820
the idea is that any model

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:03.620
that is a linear combination of
features

00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:17.840
can be fit using multiple

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.460
linear regression,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:30.510
meaning its optimal parameters
can come from this formula.

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:33.640
So,

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:35.380
for example,

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:40.260
consider the model H of

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:44.880
X1, X2

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:50.640
is equal to W0 plus W1

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:54.780
sine of the product of my two
features

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:58.860
plus w2

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:04.440
first feature raised to the
power of 17 plus

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:08.880
w3 e to the second feature

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:13.940
this seems totally random

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:19.800
but if you look at it it really
is just a sum of terms of the

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:22.860
form parameter times feature.

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:30.030
All of the w's just exist up
front.

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.480
This is a sum of w's times
things.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.980
And these things would all just
go in my design matrix.

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:42.360
So the design matrix here would
have four columns.

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.480
The first column would have a
bunch of 1's.

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:49.040
The next column would have the
signs of my original two

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:50.460
features being multiplied.

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:53.820
The next column would have my
original first feature, all of

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.020
those values raised to the power
of 17.

00:31:56.020 --> 00:31:59.380
And the next column would have
all of my second features

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:01.420
exponentiated on e.

00:32:01.420 --> 00:32:02.260
So the idea is,

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:06.640
as long as I can separate the
parameters and features in this

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:08.720
sort of linear combination way,

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:13.440
then I can use x transpose x
inverse x transpose y to find

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:15.200
the optimal parameters.

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:18.200
So this is good.

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:22.840
An example of a model that
doesn't work

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:27.340
is one where you have two
features

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:33.220
and you return something like w0
plus sine of

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:34.720
w1 x.

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:40.370
Why is that not good?

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.840
That is, why can you not use
linear regression to find the

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:47.340
optimal parameter?

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.000
But you could in the first case,

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:51.460
which looked just as
complicated.

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:52.460
Yeah?

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:57.940
Because the parameter w1 is not
scaling by the same factor for

00:32:57.940 --> 00:32:59.900
each xi?

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:03.040
Right, or it's what I think
you're getting at is the w1 is

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:04.080
within the sign.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:04.740
Yeah.

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:07.300
Right.
we need the parameters to be in

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:08.760
front of the features.

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:12.280
And the reason for that is if
you think back to how this

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:15.520
prediction vector is produced,

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.280
it takes our design matrix,
which doesn't have any

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:22.360
parameters in it, and multiplies
it by a thing that just has the

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:23.360
parameters.

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:27.040
And when I multiply design
matrix by parameter vector,

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:30.780
it puts these parameters in
front of the features.

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:31.600
Okay,

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:34.220
it doesn't like there's no way
to multiply matrix X by

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:38.540
parameter vector W and that
process puts the W's within a

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:40.860
sign or something like that,
right?

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.400
Remember X W is always a linear
combination of columns.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:47.800
So a multiple linear regression
model must always be a linear

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:50.050
combination of features.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:58.560
So here w1 is within sine

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:00.160
bad.

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:03.000
Okay?

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:07.140
So while this is called linear
regression, it can be used to

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:12.700
