00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:23.480
The final thing I want to
mention here is how we go about

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:27.230
including day of the week

00:42:29.060 --> 00:42:30.580
as an input variable.

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:34.330
How would we do that?

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:35.940
Well,

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:41.540
the naive thing to do would be
to replace every day of the week

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:42.480
with a number,

00:42:42.480 --> 00:42:46.230
like Monday equals 1, Tuesday
equals 2, Wednesday equals 3.

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:50.020
What's wrong with doing that?

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:54.520
Why should we not do that?

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:02.480
What would be wrong with that?

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:02.980
Yeah?

00:43:02.980 --> 00:43:06.730
If you're saying that Sunday
equals 7 and Monday equals 1,

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.000
then you're saying that they're
very far apart when they're not

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:11.200
actually far apart.

00:43:11.200 --> 00:43:12.700
So that's related to it.
Were you going to add to that? I

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:14.800
was going to say it implies a
mathematical relationship

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.300
between them.
Exactly.

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:20.120
It's saying that Tuesday is more
of something than Monday, and

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:21.840
Wednesday is triple Monday,

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:23.920
and Sunday is seven times
Monday.

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:27.020
But in reality, these are just
different categories.

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:29.620
It seems like there is a default
ordering to Monday, Tuesday,

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:31.820
Wednesday, the order in which
they appear in the week.

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:35.000
But what if this problem
extended to colors or something?

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:36.380
How would you sort those?

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:39.040
And you shouldn't make an
arbitrary choice, because

00:43:39.040 --> 00:43:41.200
different choices would lead to
different predictions.

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:44.360
So what we will do is a
technique

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:46.610
called one-hot encoding.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:53.780
What this does is it produces a
binary column,

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:58.500
one for each unique value of the
categorical column.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.380
So if we had five days of the
week,

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:02.360
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,
Thursday, Friday,

00:44:02.360 --> 00:44:06.110
we produce five binary columns.

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:08.580
One for whether or not the day
is Monday,

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:10.380
one for whether or not the day
is Tuesday,

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:11.760
Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday.

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:14.420
It looks like all the Wednesdays
and Fridays are zero,

00:44:14.420 --> 00:44:16.880
but that's just because in this
preview of the first three and

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:19.340
last three rows, there are no
Wednesdays or Fridays.

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:22.180
If none of them were Wednesday
or Friday, we wouldn't have had

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:24.000
a column for them because we
wouldn't have known that that

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:25.140
was a possibility,

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:27.380
if you think about this more
generally.

00:44:27.380 --> 00:44:28.820
So that's the solution.

00:44:28.820 --> 00:44:32.620
And so each of these categories
will have its own parameter.

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:36.660
And this implies lots of
interesting geometric properties

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:38.300
of the resulting model.

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:40.940
So one of the final question in
