00:53:31.940 --> 00:53:37.180
polynomial to the humble simple
linear regression than mine.

00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:52.760
Which one has a lower mean
squared error on the data that

00:53:52.760 --> 00:53:56.510
is drawn on the screen?

00:54:01.380 --> 00:54:03.560
Which one has a lower mean
squared error? It's not a trick

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:04.560
question.

00:54:06.440 --> 00:54:11.260
Yeah, the degree 10 polynomial.
It definitely passes through the

00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:15.240
blue points more closely than
the simple linear regression.

00:54:15.850 --> 00:54:17.720
Now why is this?

00:54:17.720 --> 00:54:20.860
Well, if you think about it from
the perspective

00:54:20.860 --> 00:54:26.260
of the projection of the y
vector onto the column space of

00:54:26.260 --> 00:54:26.800
x,

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:32.680
if these new columns didn't help
us make a

00:54:32.680 --> 00:54:36.940
better estimation of y using the
columns of x,

00:54:36.940 --> 00:54:40.200
these optimal parameters could
have just been set equal to 0.

00:54:41.140 --> 00:54:44.400
The only case in which they're
not set equal to 0 is if it

00:54:44.400 --> 00:54:46.740
further decreases the mean
squared error,

00:54:46.740 --> 00:54:50.760
if it allows us to bring our
prediction vector, p,

00:54:50.760 --> 00:54:52.480
closer to y.

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:54.940
There's nothing forcing these to
be non-zero.

00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:58.180
These could be 0 if these
additional higher order features

00:54:58.180 --> 00:54:59.680
did nothing.

00:55:00.140 --> 00:55:06.040
So adding more features doesn't
hurt your

00:55:06.040 --> 00:55:07.380
model's mean squared error.

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:08.700
But there's a caveat.

00:55:08.700 --> 00:55:11.440
It doesn't hurt your model's
mean squared error

00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:13.980
on the data you use to train the
model.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:17.120
That is, on your training data.

00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:20.140
So on this data that we're
