00:55:59.060 --> 00:55:59.500
Yeah?

00:55:59.500 --> 00:56:01.860
Can you overfit to the training
data?

00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:03.360
Yes, exactly.

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:10.180
So adding more features

00:56:10.180 --> 00:56:15.480
can cause overfitting.

00:56:22.520 --> 00:56:24.840
And what would that mean if
we're overfit to the training

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:25.400
data,

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:30.650
what is the risk of that, or
what is the harm of that?

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:32.560
Yeah?

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:36.700
We want it to generalize well on
unseen data.

00:56:36.700 --> 00:56:39.340
Exactly.
The whole point here is

00:56:39.340 --> 00:56:42.900
hopefully this model that we
train on our training data will

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:45.200
perform well when we don't
already know the right answer.

00:56:45.200 --> 00:56:47.080
We want it to generalize well.

00:56:47.080 --> 00:56:49.520
And if we make it hyperspecific
to the data we've already

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:50.460
collected,

00:56:50.460 --> 00:56:53.040
sure, that'll drive the mean
squared error down on the data

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:55.360
we've collected.
But we ultimately don't really

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:56.600
